Faith comes from the Latin word *fidere:*

*fidere -* to trust

&amp;#x200B;

God came due to an annotation to what it typically is stated for. You put faith IN God. God isn't put into faith. You can have faith in yourself right?

&amp;#x200B;

From definition 2, that states, you put a strong belief in God or in the doctrines of a religion in yourself. Which makes sense. However, this was not the original intention of the word. That is definition one. To trust yourself. However let's just substitute for the sake of the argument.

&amp;#x200B;

Confidence/trust = faith according to definition one. So I can have confidence that evolution is right. This is based off of scientific/logical explanations as to how old things are similar in DNA and look (though not always) but yet are extinct, or the thing closest to them lives due to a benefit the other animal did not have due to our knowledge. Also due to features we do not use but have. I.E. webbing in hands, other humans having the ability to hold their breath for a time way longer than the average human. The ability for Sherpas to live above 12,000ft. This is all evidence.

&amp;#x200B;

With religion, you have a book, no other conditions are met for logic there. You believe a man who may or may not of lived 4000+ years ago lives in the sky, which we have visited and is space now. This man controls all things, has an all seeing eye, and always is looking upon you. Dictates everything that goes on in the universe and also houses every dead soul for everything in the planet that is good. He has an army of winged ex-humans who all are destined to one day suck up the good humans still alive and battle a red demon with horns who lives beneath the Earth.

&amp;#x200B;

NONE of that is logical, I don't care who the hell you talk to. No evidence, only speculation and two books based of scraps of paper that "prophets" wrote and retold (New Testament) and ancient stories of genealogy that told us people used to live to be 900.

&amp;#x200B;

The cool part about science is we don't have all the answers. We don't really know how the origin of life was created, nobody ever will, but we have a damn good hypothesis based off observations and facts (fossils we carbon date, etc.)

&amp;#x200B;

Also how the f\*\*\* do you go to having evolution as a religion? Like come on man/woman. You are trying too hard to connect invisible dots.

&amp;#x200B;

Edit: Formatting and typos.

Evolution isn't a religion just because you put confidence/trust/faith in it. That faith is based off evidence we have seen. Evolution isn't about the origins of life. I don't know how that started and you are correct. Nobody really does. We do however know that evolution is a thin because we have strong evidence it has happened, as stated above.

&amp;#x200B;

Putting faith in science is WAY different than religion. Science does not have giant loopholes in its logic. We know things. Lots of things. Just because we don't know a lot also doesn't mean the stuff we don't know is wrong. Religion is the opposite of this in almost every way.

&amp;#x200B;

We don't know 90% of what has went on in the past to be true. Hell, there are religions that people have made up in the past 200 years we are super skeptic about but people follow blindly because a book states to (Looking at the Mormons). Written in 1830.

&amp;#x200B;

I guess this sums down to blind faith. If you are willing to put your confidence in books written by people you have never met/probably will never meet from time spans well before you were alive solely on the fact that others do so and the inherent message seems good. I won't stop you. Just stating, faith isn't always blind and is based off of fact and confidence that the knowledge behind the subject you are putting your confidence/trust into is logical. 

&amp;#x200B;

Not everything can be found out, and that means I don't put my faith in things that don't have truths/answers to yet. I.E. the edge of the universe, black holes (still a theory but we think based on hard evidence they exist), the origination of life on Earth (btw I NEVER stated I knew/had faith in anything revolving around that. Evolution = the start of life. I believe things evolve, but I don't know how it started), the origination of the Universe, if there is more than one Universe.

&amp;#x200B;

All things I don't put faith in because there isn't enough logic/knowledge to back it up. I also don't think my "faith"/"Religion Faith" is BS. It is literally two different contexts, one created due to the one context being associated with it in society more often.

I agree with you completely and I appreciate your full honesty.

&amp;#x200B;

You are correct in the way I think. I am not saying I don't want you to have those two answers, I just think they can be achieved without religion. One problem I always have is people think I can't be a good person when I don't believe in religion. That is the thing I find most hard to swallow. The second about life origins, nobody really known so whatever you choose to belief, you are putting yourself in a position where you are having blind faith because nobody really knows. The other option is to just keep thinking about finding the truth. I don't have a belief in how life came to be. I just don't think based off the evidence given and my own observation we have found the answer. So I don't have a belief on that, I just keep trying to see if we eventually do.

&amp;#x200B;

My goal in life isn't to find that belief however so that stinks but I don't let things like that rile me up.

Science in and of itself, the scientific theory, the way we produce results, the laws of physics we know, the foundation science is built off of, complex mathematics, measurements, the knowledge of waves, wavelengths, the way light moves, evolution, modern medicine, electronics, conductivity, force, gravity, etc. has very few loopholes for what we know. The fact a subject has had this much discussion and we have found out so much is incredible. The foundation of science has no loopholes because it can be figured out with science. Our reasoning/logic is not flawed currently. We just do not have the capacity to study things which we have little evidence to study.

&amp;#x200B;

How did the split of plants and animals happen? IDK, that's like locking me in a box and asking what the world is doing. I don't have enough evidence to state an answer. Same thing with the cosmos. We don't understand 95% of it because we only live and can observe .04% (not actual percentage just referencing out scale). You can't ask me for answers to questions I cannot even observe. We know plants and animals share very little in terms of structure genetically. However, they do share some key characteristics of life on Earth. Therefore, we infer that they probably split at some point from a common ancestor. Makes logical sense right? Ok, cool, I won't put any trust in that because it could be false, but that makes logical sense to me. What happens at the speed of light and why do the laws of physics stop there? I have no idea because we will probably never be able to reach the speed of light or even come close. So we just speculate. We don't put faith in those speculations, we just speculate and try to see if those speculations are correct. I.E. the scientific theory. Find the hypothesis first, then act on an experiment to see what happens.

&amp;#x200B;

It isn't "Oh, these two dudes on 07 A.D. said Jesus rose to the heavens. For all we know, his grave was robbed and these two dudes made this up. BLIND faith/confidence vs. faith/confidence from logical observations and test.

&amp;#x200B;

"put your confidence in books written by people you have never  met/probably will never meet from time spans well before you were alive  solely on the fact that others do so and the inherent message seems  good"

&amp;#x200B;

"Do you put your confidence in Darwin? Have you met most Scientific authors?  You know and met Peter Higgs?"

&amp;#x200B;

Here is the thing though, you don't know what I do and don't put my faith in. I don't look at everything I have read from somebody with a PHD and think it is fact. You have to observe yourself. Darwin had flaws in his early theory of evolution. Same way Gregor Mendel wasn't 100% right on his study of pea plants. Do I believe in recessive traits though? Yes. Why? Because I have seen it observed and that observation has been backed up by studies. Yes, those studies weren't by me, but the cool part is a lot of scientific publishing has to have documentation and must show results of studies. It isn't a self publishing scenario where you say you found historical texts and wrote a book. Will I meet those people? Absolutely not. Do I immediately put my faith in them. No, I must do my own inferring and make my own judgment. When 2000 people though all come to the same conclusion across multiple experiments across multiple lifetimes and logical thinking, I sometimes take their word for it.

&amp;#x200B;

"Bottom line is that everyone has 'Faith' in what they believe to be  true, and that is a fact. If you want to call what you believe in  "Magic", have at it Harry Potter."

&amp;#x200B;

You just stated that people put faith in what they believe to be true. I.E. confidence. I don't believe in religion. I am stating what you believe is considered "magic" and I only use the term "magic" because the others were doing so, so I thought it would be easier for you to correlate with. If it was me wording it, I would say religion, in the sense of a monotheist or polytheistic God(s) worshipping religion primarily, are made up of fake stories and lies to try to influence the people if follows to become unified and bolster it's premises. Those premises being to shun others, live a certain way, or to belief in certain stories to align a populace with certain ethics. No magic from me, that was the other people who comment. I think it isn't true, now this isn't to be mistaken to say it is bad, there is some good that comes out of religions. However there is also a lot of bad. The bad mainly is due to a lack of open mindedness and a lack of acceptance in the masses (the opposite of what most religions preach) so in reality, the problem I have with religion is more a problem with the humans who practice it more so than the religion itself (most of the time).

&amp;#x200B;

Just because something is unknown doesn't mean people put their faith in it though. We just don't believe it until we find evidence and answers.

&amp;#x200B;

Edit: This is my last post in this argument. I feel I have explained my side well and I feel this post was redundant. Nonetheless, I wish you luck in life, and I hope you find happiness! The religion/faith vs no religion/faith elsewhere argument has been going on for a while.

Moto Z2 Force Constantly restarting
So, as the title suggests, I have a Motorola Z2 Force. No problems with it up until this week. I am having what seems like hourly resets, that currently have been happening about every 3 minutes or so of using the phone.

&amp;#x200B;

I downloaded the Blades app, and this started to happen but probably uncorrelated.

&amp;#x200B;

Problems:

&amp;#x200B;

Phone, sitting or being used, will randomly reset. Go to the Motorola Logo and just sit there, sometimes reboot. Doesn't matter the battery percentage, the phone will keep the percentage it had. Could reboot at 13%, or 93%. Random.

&amp;#x200B;

 It is under warranty but I cannot get a phone that will be usable while this is in for repair/replacement for 2 weeks probably. 

&amp;#x200B;

I have factory reset the phone and this is what made it go from hourly to every 5 minutes. I had a Moto mod pack on it 24/7 but it was set to come on at 80% battery. I couldn't imagine that could cause the battery to fail because it is a certified Motorola product but you never know.

&amp;#x200B;

Any suggestions? Ill boot it again into safe mode and see if the problem persists but it looks like I will be coughing up 25 bucks for their replacement program to get a replacement phone while this gets troubleshooted. Sucky part is redoing all of my Google authentication crap. Takes so long to disable then re-enable.

&amp;#x200B;

I am guessing it is a crapped out battery/battery connection. The phone doesn't allow the battery to be taken out so I can't troubleshoot there.

This is about the best way you can sum it up I think.

