Crappy photo I made in GIMP to illustrate. I work at a small office and they have had a checkpoint wifi router for a long time. This was seemingly hooked up to a switch and that switch hooking everything else up. Comcast came in and put their own router in but left everything else hooked up. Great no issues. Me and a co-worker had some issues with a certain Intel Wifi reciever device on our laptops not connecting to the Checkpoint, no biggie. So they ask me to go out and get a new wifi transmitter/router and hook it up. EZPZ.

&amp;#x200B;

My question is, I want to get rid of some redundancy here and it is a mess. They would like the old checkpoint gone but they didn't want to pay for a new router with a DMZ port. No biggie, I can configure that in the D-Link I setup.  However, I don't understand quite well how everything is hooking up and we use our Windows 2012 Server a good bit, as we have AD accounts people use to login to their computers with. We also used to have out own exchange server before we moved to using GoDaddy and Exchange itself for our email.

&amp;#x200B;

So, I wired the D-Link up to the switch which is connected to the Checkpoint, which is connected to the router, which is all connected to the R320 Server. The switch is also where the SQL desktop is connected. This is good because it makes the D-Link have access to the other things on our network, this being a big deal as we are a Copier Company and I can have anywhere from 2-10 copiers hooked up to this network for demonstrations.

&amp;#x200B;

Problems: I have access to the SQL windows Server (which has a companyname.local attached to it so I assume this is running on the R320) (I am trying to find that out will update) but I do not have access the the VMware login credentials for the R320 itself. I also do not have the login credentials for the Checkpoint itself. Both of those reasons are scaring me from unplugging things because I have 0 and I mean 0 knowledge of this hardware and its setup prior to coming on to the job about a year ago. The checkpoint has been out of service three years and that is when the last IT guy came for the server and what not.

&amp;#x200B;

Could I just unplug the Checkpoint router, wire the server into the DMZ port on the D-Link and just set it up to whatever makes sense and troubleshoot from there? Or is it suicide to go at that in the dark not knowing what the VMware needs in terms of DMZ setup and whether of not the D-Link can do all it needs to do?

&amp;#x200B;

TLDR: What in this can I get rid of while keeping the D-Link and not knowing where the Windows Server 2012 is stored (I am trying to find that out will update), not knowing the Checkpoint login information, and not knowing the R320 VMware login information.

