I won't lie and tell you I have been a nomad for X years and have this figured out.

&amp;#x200B;

I am 22 right now and would love to be in your position. However, I am not, but I would like to give you my input nonetheless and I hope you see it!

&amp;#x200B;

At 250k/yr you are saving 100k (let's assume USD). Thats 150k/yr you are spending. Not gonna lie, from living at 34k before taxes for over a year not and living with my parents, I still struggle a little. The QOL in almost any city except Silicon Valey, Seattle, and maybe Austin/Dallas is exceptional at that kind of money.

&amp;#x200B;

If I were you, I wouldn't quit. Not yet. Just because you have the 2 attached to your age doesn't mean shit in my opinion. How are you doing? Are you healthy? Are you outdoor/travel minded? Have you done it a lot? Do you go hiking/camping a lot? Have you worked on automobiles before?

&amp;#x200B;

There is a lot of experience I would feel I would like to have just to shortcut the trouble on the road. Say your can/expedition vehicle breaks down, can you replace a coil pack? Spark plugs? Not hating if you can easily do these things, it is just that you can live super comfortably (and I would want to make sure I got the wants of that kind of lifestyle out of the way before I gave it up). You make a lot at a low stress job, that leaves the perfect time to explore areas around where you live, or be a weekend warrior and fly to tons of different places and travel that way before you give it up for good (again, possibly).

&amp;#x200B;

Real estate investment is a good idea from what I have collected to get into, preferably with a partner who can fix things up and wants to do it, where you front some money and collect X percent a month. Helps take off the stress of money. I would also just take the time to build my overland rig while you work. No sense in not doing that while you are financially able. Make it your dream rig. Brings the excitement of travel still without fully committing.

&amp;#x200B;

Just my .02 cents. You can have the sense of freedom t an extent/be excited for it to soon come without having to give up your lifestyle. Make sure everything is set up when you quick. Steady secondary streams of income, etc. Makes it even better. I just wouldn't rush anything. 28 is just a number. I just turned 22 last week, and it felt like a normal thursday. :D

&amp;#x200B;

Good luck my friend! Whatever you do, remember you did it because it was the best thing for you at the time!

